W="wiki-Rigoletto.txt"
mk("rigoletto","Rigoletto",["Rigoletto"],W,[
("premiere",None,"Rigoletto had a triumphant premiere at La Fenice in Venice on 11 March 1851, after serious trouble with the Austrian censors.","the opera had a triumphant premiere at La Fenice in Venice on 11 March 1851"),
("history",None,"The source, Victor Hugo\u2019s play Le roi s\u2019amuse, had been banned in France after its premiere and wasn\u2019t staged again until 1882.","The play had been banned in France following its premiere nearly twenty years earlier (not to be staged again until 1882)","Composition_history"),
("composer",None,"Verdi told Piave to get permission at any cost: \u201cUse four legs, run through the town and find me an influential person.\u201d","Use four legs, run through the town and find me an influential person","Composition_history","Verdi, letter to Piave (via Wikipedia)"),
("composer",None,"Verdi on his hunchbacked jester: \u201cThere is a character that is one of the greatest creations that the theatre can boast of.\u201d","there is a character that is one of the greatest creations that the theatre can boast of","Composition_history","Verdi (via Wikipedia)"),
("history",None,"The censor flatly refused \u201cLa Maledizione\u201d (the working title). One rewrite turned the king into a duke and dropped both the hunchback and the curse; Verdi refused it.",["the Austrian censor De Gorzkowski emphatically denied consent to the production of \"La Maledizione\"","in which the sovereign was a duke and both the hunchback and the curse disappeared","Verdi was completely against this proposed solution"],"Composition_history"),
("history",None,"The jester was originally called Triboulet. The name Rigoletto came from a parody comedy, \u201cRigoletti, or The last of the jesters\u201d.","renamed Rigoletto from a parody of a comedy","Composition_history"),
("blooper","Della mia bella|Questa o quella|^Act I$","At the premiere, Felice Varesi (Rigoletto) froze in panic. Verdi pushed him onstage, and he tumbled on clumsily; the audience thought it was a planned gag.",["he had a panic attack when it was his turn to enter the stage","roughly pushed him on the stage, so he appeared with a clumsy tumble","The audience, thinking it was an intentional gag, was very amused."],"19th-century_productions"),
("composer","La donna \u00e8 mobile|La donna e mobile","Verdi kept \u201cLa donna \u00e8 mobile\u201d secret until a few hours before the premiere and forbade anyone to sing or whistle it outside the theatre.","forbidding them to sing, whistle or even think of the melody outside of the theatre","19th-century_productions"),
("premiere","La donna \u00e8 mobile|La donna e mobile","The morning after the premiere, \u201cLa donna \u00e8 mobile\u201d was being sung in the streets.","which was sung in the streets the next morning","19th-century_productions"),
("history",None,"Because of censorship, Rigoletto sometimes played in Italy under other names, such as Viscardello, Lionello and Clara de Perth.","e.g. as Viscardello, Lionello, and Clara de Perth","19th-century_productions"),
("music","Zitti, zitti","\u201cZitti, zitti\u201d (\u201cSoftly, softly\u201d): blindfolded and tricked, Rigoletto holds the ladder while the courtiers abduct his own daughter.","Blindfolded and deceived, he holds the ladder steady while they climb up to Gilda's room","Act_1"),
("music","Scorrendo uniti","The courtiers gleefully tell the Duke they have captured \u201cRigoletto\u2019s mistress\u201d. It is really Gilda, his daughter.","they have captured Rigoletto's mistress","Act_2"),
("history",None,"Verdi finished the score on 5 February 1851, little more than a month before the premiere.","Verdi finally completed the composition on 5 February 1851, a little more than a month before the premiere.","Composition_history"),
])
